          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When one code already covers both a condition and its manifestation, is it wrong to add a second code for the manifestation?",
          "answer": "**It depends on the convention, so adding a second code is not automatically wrong.** The retrieved coding guidance supports this general picture:\n\n- **Combination codes** pack two conditions, or a condition and its complication, into one code. Where one exists, it is used *instead of* separate codes for the same pair. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- **\"Code first\" and \"use additional code\"** work as a pair. The underlying condition is reported first, and a second code is then added for the manifestation or detail. (Same source.)\n- **Some combination codes still need a second code.** In diabetes, the combination code names the link, and an additional code gives the detail, such as the stage of kidney disease or the site and depth of a foot ulcer. (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*.)\n- **Heart failure** works the same way. When hypertensive heart disease is documented, the combination code is reported first, and a heart failure code is *added* to identify the type. (*Coding heart failure: I50*.)\n\nSo the rule is not \"one code covers it, so never add another.\" Check the instruction attached to the code. If it says to use an additional code, adding one is correct.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not explain the dagger-asterisk (etiology/manifestation) convention in detail, including which symbol marks which code and how the two codes are sequenced. I can't say more about that from these sources. A medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list and guidelines would be the right place to confirm the exact rule for a specific case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
